ith |p+q|=2 can have at most floor((k+1)/2) points. The bound is sharp if there exists a line with exactly floor((k+1)/2) points. The solution claims only three lines achieve that bound. But we need to verify if lines with other slopes can also achieve that bound for some k.\n\nLet's test for k=5 (floor((k+1)/2) = 3). We saw that slope -1/3 line (p=-1,q=3) gave at most 2 points. So not achieving bound. For k=7 (floor((k+1)/2) = 4). Let's test if slope -1/3 line can have 4 points in T_7. T_7 includes points (x,y) with x+y ≤ 8. Let's list points for T_7: total points = 7*8/2 = 28. We need to see if line x + 3y = C can have 4 points. Let's compute C values for points in T_7.\n\nPoints (x,y) with x+y ≤ 8, x,y≥1. Let's generate all points and compute C = x + 3y. We'll need to see if any C appears at least 4 times.\n\nBetter to think combinatorially: For a given C, the points satisfying x + 3y = C are integer solutions with x = C - 3y, y≥1, x≥1, and also x+y ≤ k+1 = 8. So we need y≥1, x≥1 => C - 3y ≥ 1 => y ≤ (C-1)/3. Also x+y = C - 3y + y = C - 2y ≤ 8 => 2y ≥ C - 8 => y ≥ (C-8)/2. So y must satisfy max(1, ceil((C-8)/2)) ≤ y ≤ floor((C-1)/3). The number of integer y in that interval is the number of points.\n\nWe need to find C such that the interval length is at least 4. Let's try to find C values.\n\nWe can brute compute for C from minimal possible to maximal possible. Minimal C: smallest x+y is 2 (x=1,y=1) => C = 1 + 3*1 = 4. Max C: largest x+y is 8, but x and y can be up to 7? Actually max x is 7 (when y=1) => C = 7 + 3*1 = 10. Or max y is 7 (when x=1) => C = 1 + 3*7 = 22. So C ranges from 4 to 22.\n\nLet's compute for each C the number of solutions.\n\nWe can do a quick enumeration manually or programmatically in mind. Let's do manually.\n\nWe need integer y≥1, x = C - 3y ≥ 1, and x+y ≤ 8.\n\nWe can iterate y from 1 up to maybe floor((C-1)/3). For each y, compute x = C - 3y, check x≥1 and x+y ≤ 8.\n\nLet's do for each C:\n\nC=4: y=1 => x=1 => x+y=2 ≤8 => valid. y=2 => x= -2 (invalid). So 1 point.\n\nC=5: y=1 => x=2 => x+y=3 ≤8 => valid. y=2 => x= -1 invalid. So 1 point.\n\nC=6: y=1 => x=3 => sum=4 ≤8 => valid. y=2 => x=0 invalid. So 1 point.\n\nC=7: y=1 => x=4 => sum=5 ≤8 => valid. y=2 => x=1 => sum=3 ≤8 => valid. y=3 => x=-2 invalid. So 2 points: (4,1),(1,2).\n\nC=8: y=1 => x=5 => sum=6 ≤8 => valid. y=2 => x=2 => sum=4 ≤8 => valid. y=3 => x=-1 invalid. So 2 points: (5,1),(2,2).\n\nC=9: y=1 => x=6 => sum=7 ≤8 => valid. y=2 => x=3 => sum=5 ≤8 => valid. y=3 => x=0 invalid. So 2 points: (6,1),(3,2).\n\nC=10: y=1 => x=7 => sum=8 ≤8 => valid. y=2 => x=4 => sum=6 ≤8 => valid. y=3 => x=1 => sum=4 ≤8 => valid. y=4 => x=-2 invalid. So 3 points: (7,1),(4,2),(1,3). Check (1,3): sum=4 ≤8, okay.\n\nC=11: y=1 => x=8 => sum=9 >8 => invalid (x+y >8). So y=1 invalid. y=2 => x=5 => sum=7 ≤8 => valid. y=3 => x=2 => sum=5 ≤8 => valid. y=4 => x=-1 invalid. So 2 points: (5,2),(2,3). Check (5,2): sum=7 ≤8, okay.\n\nC=12: y=1 => x=9 => sum=10 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=6 => sum=8 ≤8 => valid. y=3 => x=3 => sum=6 ≤8 => valid. y=4 => x=0 invalid. So 2 points: (6,2),(3,3).\n\nC=13: y=1 => x=10 => sum=11 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=7 => sum=9 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=4 => sum=7 ≤8 => valid. y=4 => x=1 => sum=5 ≤8 => valid. y=5 => x=-2 invalid. So 2 points: (4,3),(1,4).\n\nC=14: y=1 => x=11 => sum=12 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=8 => sum=10 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=5 => sum=8 ≤8 => valid. y=4 => x=2 => sum=6 ≤8 => valid. y=5 => x=-1 invalid. So 2 points: (5,3),(2,4).\n\nC=15: y=1 => x=12 => sum=13 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=9 => sum=11 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=6 => sum=9 >8 invalid. y=4 => x=3 => sum=7 ≤8 => valid. y=5 => x=0 invalid. So 1 point: (3,4). Wait also y=6 => x=-3 invalid. So only (3,4). So 1 point.\n\nC=16: y=1 => x=13 => sum=14 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=10 => sum=12 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=7 => sum=10 >8 invalid. y=4 => x=4 => sum=8 ≤8 => valid. y=5 => x=1 => sum=6 ≤8 => valid. y=6 => x=-2 invalid. So 2 points: (4,4),(1,5). Check (4,4): sum=8, okay.\n\nC=17: y=1 => x=14 => sum=15 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=11 => sum=13 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=8 => sum=11 >8 invalid. y=4 => x=5 => sum=9 >8 invalid. y=5 => x=2 => sum=7 ≤8 => valid. y=6 => x=-1 invalid. So 1 point: (2,5).\n\nC=18: y=1 => x=15 => sum=16 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=12 => sum=14 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=9 => sum=12 >8 invalid. y=4 => x=6 => sum=10 >8 invalid. y=5 => x=3 => sum=8 ≤8 => valid. y=6 => x=0 invalid. So 1 point: (3,5).\n\nC=19: y=1 => x=16 => sum=17 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=13 => sum=15 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=10 => sum=13 >8 invalid. y=4 => x=7 => sum=11 >8 invalid. y=5 => x=4 => sum=9 >8 invalid. y=6 => x=1 => sum=7 ≤8 => valid. So 1 point: (1,6).\n\nC=20: y=1 => x=17 => sum=18 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=14 => sum=16 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=11 => sum=14 >8 invalid. y=4 => x=8 => sum=12 >8 invalid. y=5 => x=5 => sum=10 >8 invalid. y=6 => x=2 => sum=8 ≤8 => valid. y=7 => x=-1 invalid. So 1 point: (2,6).\n\nC=21: y=1 => x=18 => sum=19 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=15 => sum=17 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=12 => sum=15 >8 invalid. y=4 => x=9 => sum=13 >8 invalid. y=5 => x=6 => sum=11 >8 invalid. y=6 => x=3 => sum=9 >8 invalid. y=7 => x=0 invalid. So 0 points.\n\nC=22: y=1 => x=19 => sum=20 >8 invalid. y=2 => x=16 => sum=18 >8 invalid. y=3 => x=13 => sum=16 >8 invalid. y=4 => x=10 => sum=14 >8 invalid. y=5 => x=7 => sum=12 >8 invalid. y=6 => x=4 => sum=10 >8 invalid. y=7 => x=1 => sum=8 ≤8 => valid. So 1 point: (1,7).\n\nThus the maximum number of points on any line x+3y=C is 3 (for C=10 gave 3 points). So slope -1/3 line can have at most 3 points for k=7, but floor((k+1)/2) = 4. So it cannot achieve the bound. So indeed slope -1/3 cannot achieve the bound for any k≥3? Let's test for larger k. The bound is floor((k+1)/2). For a line with p+q=2, the number of points is at most floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). The bound is achieved only if the line can have exactly floor((k+1)/2) points. For p+q=2, the line's points are given by (x0+qt, y0+pt) with p+q=2. The sum x+y = (x0+y0)+(p+q)t = (x0+y0)+2t. So the sum changes by 2 each step. So the points in T_k have sum between 2 and k+1. So the number of integer t such that sum is within that range is at most floor((k+1 - (x0+y0))/2) - ceil((2 - (x0+y0))/2) + 1. The maximum possible is when (x0+y0) is as small as possible (i.e., 2) and as large as possible (i.e., maybe 2?). Actually to maximize number of points, we want the smallest possible starting sum (x0+y0) such that the line passes through a point in T_k. The smallest sum possible is 2 (point (1,1)). If the line passes through (1,1), then x0+y0 = 2. Then the sum values are 2 + 2t. For t≥0, sum = 2,4,6,... up to ≤ k+1. So number of points = floor((k+1 - 2)/2) + 1 = floor((k-1)/2) + 1 = floor((k+1)/2). So the bound is achieved if there is a line with p+q=2 that passes through (1,1). Does any such line exist? Let's check.\n\nWe need p x - q y = c, with p+q=2, and (1,1) satisfies: p*1 - q*1 = c => c = p - q. Since p+q=2, we have p = 2 - q. So c = (2 - q) - q = 2 - 2q = 2(1 - q). So c is even. So the line is (2 - q) x - q y = 2(1 - q). Let's see if this line is integer and passes through other points in T_k. For q=1, p=1, c=0 => line x - y = 0 => y = x, passes through (1,1). That's slope 1 line. For q=3, p=-1, c = 2(1 - 3) = -4 => line -x - 3y = -4 => x + 3y = 4. Does this line pass through (1,1)? Check: 1 + 3*1 = 4, yes. So line x + 3y = 4 passes through (1,1). But does it have slope -1/3? Yes. So this line is a candidate. Let's see how many points it contains in T_k. For k=5, we saw line x+3y=4 only contains (1,1). For k=7, line x+3y=4 contains (1,1) only (since we computed earlier for C=4 gave only (1,1)). So it doesn't achieve the bound for k≥5. Why? Because the line's points are given by (x0+qt, y0+pt) with p=-1, q=3. Starting from (1,1), the next point is (1+3, 1-1) = (4,0) which is not in T_k because y=0 not positive. So the line only has one point in the positive integer region. So the bound is not achieved because the line's step moves out of the region quickly. So the bound is only achieved for lines where the step (q,p) yields both coordinates staying positive for many steps. For p=1,q=1 (slope 1), the step is (1,1) and we get points (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),... staying positive. For p=-2,q=1 (slope -2), step is (1,-2). Starting from (1,5) maybe? Actually we need to find a line that passes through (1,5) and then (2,3) and (3,1). Indeed the line y = -2x + 7 passes through (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). The step is (1,-2). Starting from (1,5), next point (2,3) still positive, next (3,1) still positive, next (4,-1) out of region. So we get exactly 3 points. So the bound is achieved for slope -2 line.\n\nFor p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2), step is (2,-1). Starting from (1,3) we get (3,2),(5,1). So we get 3 points. So the bound is achieved for slope -1/2 line.\n\nThus only those three lines have the property that the step (q,p) yields both coordinates positive for enough steps to achieve the bound. For other p+q=2 lines, the step may be too large in one direction causing early exit from the region. So the solution's claim that only three possibilities exist is plausible, but they need to argue that only those three lines can achieve the bound. They gave a short statement: \"Equation (2.1) with |p+q|=2 gives three possibilities: p=q=1 (slope 1); p=-2,q=1 (slope -2); p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2).\" However, as we saw, there are infinitely many possibilities for p+q=2, but many are not primitive (gcd(p,q)=1) or produce horizontal lines (p=0). Actually p+q=2 yields p = 2 - q. For q>2, p negative. For q=3, p=-1, gcd( -1,3) = 1, valid. So there is a valid line with p=-1,q=3. So the solution's claim that only three possibilities exist is false. However, they might be restricting to lines that achieve the bound (i.e., have |p+q|=2 and also have the maximum number of points). But they didn't explicitly state that they are only considering lines that achieve the bound. They said \"Equation (2.1) with |p+q|=2 gives three possibilities\". That's inaccurate. So there is a factual error in the reasoning: they omitted other possibilities for p+q=2. However, does this error affect the correctness of the final answer? Possibly not, if they later argue that only those three lines can achieve the bound, but they didn't justify why other possibilities cannot achieve the bound. They just claimed \"No other sunny line can contain m+1 points, because any other choice would have |p+q|≥3 or |p+q|=1 with q≥2, and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound.\" This statement is also inaccurate because they omitted the case |p+q|=2 with other p,q values. They claim any other choice would have |p+q|≥3 or |p+q|=1 with q≥2. But we have |p+q|=2 with p=-1,q=3, which is not covered. So the reasoning is incomplete/incorrect. However, does this affect the conclusion that only k=1,3 are possible? Let's examine if there could be a construction for k=5 using lines with slopes other than the three given. We need to see if there exists a set of 5 sunny lines covering T_5. The solution claims no, because only three lines can achieve the bound, and we need k ≤ 3. But maybe there is a different arrangement of 5 sunny lines that covers T_5, not necessarily each line achieving the bound individually. The counting argument gave equality for odd k, but that only forces that each line must have exactly floor((k+1)/2) points and lines must be disjoint. So each line must have exactly (k+1)/2 points. For k=5, each line must have exactly 3 points. So we need 5 lines each containing exactly 3 points, covering all 15 points, with no overlap. The three lines we have (slope 1, -2, -1/2) each contain 3 points, but they only cover 9 points. So we need two more lines each containing 3 points, disjoint from the existing ones and covering the remaining 6 points. Are there other lines that contain exactly 3 points of T_5? Let's search.\n\nWe need lines with integer coefficients p,q,c, gcd(p,q)=1, p≠0, p≠ -q, q>0, that intersect T_5 in exactly 3 points. Let's enumerate all possible lines that intersect T_5 in at least 2 points, then see if any have exactly 3 points.\n\nWe can brute think: The points of T_5 are arranged in a triangular grid. A line with integer slope (rational) can pass through multiple lattice points. The maximum number of collinear points in this triangle is known to be 3 for many slopes. For example, lines of slope 0 (horizontal) are non-sunny, but we can ignore. Slope infinite (vertical) also non-sunny. Slope -1 lines are non-sunny. So we need slopes other than 0, infinite, -1.\n\nWe can try to find lines with slope 2 (p=2,q=1). Equation y = 2x + b. For integer points, b integer. Let's see if any such line passes through 3 points in T_5. Points (x,y) with y = 2x + b, x≥1, y≥1, x+y ≤6. Let's try to find b such that there are 3 solutions.\n\nWe can solve for x: y = 2x + b => x + y = x + 2x + b = 3x + b ≤ 6 => x ≤ (6 - b)/3. Also y = 2x + b ≥ 1 => 2x + b ≥ 1 => x ≥ (1 - b)/2. Also x integer ≥1.\n\nWe can try small b values.\n\nb can be negative or positive. Let's try b = -1: y = 2x - 1. Points: x=1 => y=1 => (1,1) sum=2 ≤6. x=2 => y=3 => (2,3) sum=5 ≤6. x=3 => y=5 => (3,5) sum=8 >6, invalid. So we have (1,1),(2,3). That's 2 points.\n\nb = -2: y = 2x - 2. x=1 => y=0 invalid. So no points.\n\nb = 0: y = 2x. x=1 => y=2 => (1,2) sum=3 ≤6. x=2 => y=4 => (2,4) sum=6 ≤6. x=3 => y=6 => (3,6) sum=9 >6. So 2 points.\n\nb = -3: y = 2x - 3. x=2 => y=1 => (2,1) sum=3 ≤6. x=3 => y=3 => (3,3) sum=6 ≤6. x=4 => y=5 => (4,5) sum=9 >6. x=1 => y=-1 invalid. So 2 points.\n\nb = -4: y = 2x - 4. x=3 => y=2 => (3,2) sum=5 ≤6. x=4 => y=4 => (4,4) sum=8 >6. x=2 => y=0 invalid. So 1 point.\n\nb = -5: y = 2x -5. x=3 => y=1 => (3,1) sum=4 ≤6. x=4 => y=3 => (4,3) sum=7 >6. So 1 point.\n\nb = -6: y = 2x -6. x=4 => y=2 => (4,2) sum=6 ≤6. x=5 => y=4 => (5,4) sum=9 >6. So 1 point.\n\nb = -7: y = 2x -7. x=4 => y=1 => (4,1) sum=5 ≤6. x=5 => y=3 => (5,3) sum=8 >6. So 1 point.\n\nb = -8: y = 2x -8. x=5 => y=2 => (5,2) sum=7 >6. So none.\n\nThus slope 2 lines have at most 2 points.\n\nNow slope 1/2 (p=1,q=2). Equation y = (1/2)x + b => 2y = x + 2b => x = 2y - 2b. Let's try to find lines with 3 points. Equivalent to x - 2y = -2b. So p=1,q=2, p+q=3, not 2. So bound is floor((k+1)/3) = floor(6/3)=2 for k=5. So cannot have 3 points. So slope 1/2 lines cannot have 3 points.\n\nSlope -3 (p=-3,q=1). Equation y = -3x + b. Let's test b values.\n\nb = 8: y = -3x + 8. x=1 => y=5 => (1,5) sum=6 ≤6. x=2 => y=2 => (2,2) sum=4 ≤6. x=3 => y=-1 invalid. So 2 points.\n\nb = 7: y = -3x + 7. x=1 => y=4 => (1,4) sum=5 ≤6. x=2 => y=1 => (2,1) sum=3 ≤6. x=3 => y=-2 invalid. So 2 points.\n\nb = 9: y = -3x + 9. x=1 => y=6 => (1,6) sum=7 >6 invalid. So none.\n\nThus slope -3 lines have at most 2 points.\n\nSlope 3 (p=3,q=1). y = 3x + b. b negative likely. b = -2: y = 3x -2. x=1 => y=1 => (1,1) sum=2 ≤6. x=2 => y=4 => (2,4) sum=6 ≤6. x=3 => y=7 => sum=10 >6. So 2 points.\n\nThus many slopes have at most 2 points.\n\nThus the only slopes that can have 3 points are those with |p+q|=2 and appropriate p,q such that the step (q,p) yields both coordinates positive for at least 3 points. The only primitive pairs (p,q) with p+q=2 and q>0, gcd(p,q)=1, p≠0, p≠ -q are:\n\n- q=1, p=1 (slope 1)\n- q=3, p=-1 (slope -1/3)\n- q=4, p=-2 (but gcd(-2,4)=2, not allowed)\n- q=5, p=-3 (gcd(-3,5)=1) slope -3/5\n- q=6, p=-4 (gcd(-4,6)=2) not allowed\n- q=7, p=-5 (gcd(-5,7)=1) slope -5/7\n- q=8, p=-6 (gcd(-6,8)=2) not allowed\n- q=9, p=-7 (gcd(-7,9)=1) slope -7/9\n- etc.\n\nSimilarly for p+q = -2 (i.e., p = -2 - q). That yields p negative, q positive, but p+q = -2. Let's list possibilities:\n\n- q=1 => p=-3 (slope -3)\n- q=2 => p=-4 (gcd(-4,2)=2) not allowed\n- q=3 => p=-5 (gcd(-5,3)=1) slope -5/3\n- q=4 => p=-6 (gcd(-6,4)=2) not allowed\n- q=5 => p=-7 (gcd(-7,5)=1) slope -7/5\n- etc.\n\nBut the solution only considered p=q=1 (slope 1), p=-2,q=1 (slope -2), p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2). Wait, p=-2,q=1 corresponds to p+q = -1? Actually p=-2,q=1 => p+q = -1. That's not |p+q|=2. Wait, check: p=-2, q=1 => p+q = -1,