ve n lines total. Suppose we have k sunny lines, and n-k non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal. Each non-sunny line can cover many points in a column (vertical) or many points in a row (horizontal) or many points on a diagonal (slope -1). But we need to cover all points. The points are arranged in a triangular shape. The maximum number of points a non-sunny line can cover is n (vertical x=1, horizontal y=1, diagonal x+y=n+1). So each non-sunny line can cover up to n points. So with n-k non-sunny lines, we can cover at most (n-k)*n points. The remaining points must be covered by sunny lines. Sunny lines can each cover at most n points (since each column at most one point, there are n columns). So total coverage capacity is (n-k)*n + k*n = n*n = n^2. That's enough for N = n(n+1)/2. So capacity is not a limiting factor.\n\nBut we need to consider the distribution of points across columns. The columns have decreasing numbers of points. Non-sunny lines can cover many points in a column (vertical) or many points across columns (horizontal/diagonal). However, we need to ensure that each point is covered at least once. The main difficulty is covering the rightmost columns with many points using sunny lines, because they cannot cover multiple points in the same column. So we need enough non-sunny lines to cover the columns with many points. Let's think: The column i has n+1-i points. For i large (close to n), the column has few points. For i small (like i=1), column has n points. So we need to cover column 1 with n points. If we have k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point in column 1, so they can cover at most k points in column 1. The remaining n - k points in column 1 must be covered by non-sunny lines. Since vertical line x=1 can cover all n points, we could use one vertical line to cover column 1 entirely. So we don't need many non-sunny lines for column 1. Similarly, we could use vertical lines for some columns. So the main constraint is the number of columns that need to be covered by non-sunny lines. Since we have n-k non-sunny lines, we can cover at most n-k columns fully (by vertical lines). The remaining columns must be covered by sunny lines, each covering at most one point per column. So we need to ensure that the total number of points in those columns is ≤ k (since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover one point in each of those columns). Actually each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover points in multiple columns. So if we have k sunny lines, they can cover up to k points in each column, but we need to cover all points in those columns. However, we can assign each sunny line to cover one point in each of the columns that are not covered by vertical lines. So the total number of points that need to be covered by sunny lines across all columns not covered by vertical lines must be ≤ k * (number of such columns). But each column may have multiple points; we need to cover all points in those columns. So we need enough sunny lines to cover all points in those columns. Let's formalize.\n\nLet S be the set of columns that are not covered by a vertical line (i.e., we don't have a non-sunny line covering them). For each column i in S, we need to cover its points using sunny lines (or possibly horizontal/diagonal lines). But we have only k sunny lines, and each sunny line can intersect column i at most once. So the total number of points we can cover in column i using sunny lines is at most k (since each sunny line can give at most one point). However, column i may have more than k points. So we need to ensure that for each column i not covered by a vertical line, its number of points ≤ k. Since column i has n+1-i points, we need n+1-i ≤ k for all i in S. So the columns not covered by vertical lines must be those with at most k points. So we can choose to not use vertical lines for columns with ≤ k points. The columns with > k points must be covered by vertical lines (or other non-sunny lines). Since we have n-k non-sunny lines, we can cover at most n-k columns with vertical lines. So we need to ensure that the number of columns with > k points is ≤ n-k.\n\nNow, columns with > k points are those i such that n+1-i > k => i n+1 - k. So columns i = 1,2,..., n - k (since i n+1 - k => i ≤ n - k). So there are n - k columns with > k points. So we need at most n - k non-sunny lines to cover them. We have exactly n - k non-sunny lines. So we can assign each of those columns a vertical line. That works. So the condition is satisfied for any k? Let's check: For any k, we have n - k columns with > k points, and we have n - k non-sunny lines. So we can assign vertical lines to those columns. Then the remaining columns (i = n - k + 1,..., n) have ≤ k points each. There are k columns left (since total columns = n). Indeed, columns i = n - k + 1,..., n are exactly k columns. Each of those columns has at most k points. So we can cover all points in those columns using k sunny lines, each line covering at most one point per column. Since there are k columns and k sunny lines, we can assign each sunny line to cover one point in each column, covering all points? Wait, each column may have up to k points, but we have only k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So we can cover at most k points in each column. Since each column has at most k points, we can cover all points in each column using the k sunny lines, provided we can assign points appropriately. But we need to ensure that each point in those columns can be assigned to a distinct sunny line such that each sunny line covers at most one point per column. This is essentially a bipartite matching problem: we have k sunny lines (like \"slots\") and each column i (in S) has up to k points. We need to assign each point to a sunny line such that each line gets at most one point per column (which is automatically satisfied) but also each